Object Class in Java
-----------------------------------------------------
Object class (in java.lang) is the root of the Java class hierarchy.
Every class in Java either directly or indirectly extends Object.

👉 Object class is the parent (superclass) of all classes in Java
👉 Every class you create:
class Student {}
👉 Internally becomes:
class Student extends Object {}
-“Every object in Java is ultimately an Object class object”

🧠 Why Object class is needed (Simple Points)
Java needs one common parent for all classes
To make all objects behave in a standard way
To avoid writing same logic again and again
To allow generic handling of objects
To provide built-in common methods (equals, hashCode, etc.)

❌ Problems before Object class (imagine if not present)
No common type → cannot store different objects together
No standard methods → every class must write its own logic
Code duplication → same methods repeated in every class
Difficult to build generic features (like collections)
No uniform way to compare or print objects

🔥 How Object class helps in polymorphism
✅ Point 1: Common parent for all classes

👉 Every class extends Object

class Student extends Object
class Employee extends Object


👉 So we can write:

Object obj1 = new Student();
Object obj2 = new Employee();


✔ SAME reference, different objects

✅ Point 2: One variable → many forms
Object obj;

obj = new Student();
obj = new Employee();
obj = new Car();


👉 This is polymorphism ✔

✅ Point 3: Useful in collections
List<Object> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(new Student());
list.add(new Employee());
list.add("Hello");


👉 Different types stored together ✔

✅ Point 4: Method handling
public void print(Object obj) {
    System.out.println(obj.toString());
}


👉 Works for ANY object ✔

🧠 SIMPLE TABLE
Method	Use
equals()	compare objects
hashCode()	generate hash
toString()	print object
getClass()	class info
wait()	pause thread
notify()	wake thread
notifyAll()	wake all threads
clone()	copy object
finalize()	cleanup (deprecated)



